      "problem": "Given that the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{3}+4x$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,3],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.2$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2{x}^{3}+4x)^{′}={e}^{x}-6{x}^{2}+4, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-6{x}^{2}+4)^{′}={e}^{x}-12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-12{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6{x}_{0}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-12{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =1.2-\\frac{{e}^{1.2}-6(1.2)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.2}-12(1.2)} \\\\  & =1.080\\,875….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6{x}_{1}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-12{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =1.080\\,875…-\\frac{{e}^{1.080\\,875…}-6(1.080\\,875…)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.080\\,875…}-12(1.080\\,875…)} \\\\  & =1.074\\,640…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={x}_{2}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-6{x}_{2}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-12{x}_{2}} \\\\  & =1.074\\,640…-\\frac{{e}^{1.074\\,640…}-6(1.074\\,640…)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.074\\,640…}-12(1.074\\,640…)} \\\\  & =1.074\\,623….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.07$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.07$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.07,$ rounded to two decimal places.",
